--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` converts an enum argument by the case's own *name*, and the value it becomes is that case's backing integer — ADR 0086 § 6, `rule:enums/no-class-machinery`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6's "a matched value's type comes from the parameter", for the last type
// the argument matcher admitted and could not convert. Two decisions are
// pinned here and `nvs_types::commands::ArgConv::Enum` is the home of both: a
// command line writes the **case name**, because § 6's own refusal names every
// value it would have accepted and a list of backing integers is not a
// sentence a person can act on; and the value that word becomes is the case's
// backing integer, because an enum case *is* that integer by the time it is a
// value.
//
// The backing here is deliberately not the case's position: `Warn` is 10, so a
// conversion that handed back an index rather than a value still prints
// plausibly on a one-case enum and fails on this one. The sibling case
// `command-run-refuses-an-enum-argument-written-as-its-backing-value` asserts
// the other side of the same decision — the number `10` is not a spelling of
// `Warn`.
enum Level: uint {
    Warn = 10,
    Error = 20,
}

class Logs {
    #[Core\Command(name: "emit", about: "Write one line at a level")]
    public static function emit(Level $level): uint {
        echo "level: ", $level as uint, "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

// The status is taken first, because the handler writes while `run` is still
// being evaluated.
uint $status = Core\Command::run();
echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
--ARGS--
emit
Error
--EXPECT--
level: 20
status: 0
